<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged by Tauri into the Info.plist it generates for the macOS bundle.

  Dictation is the webview's `webkitSpeechRecognition`, and on macOS that is
  Apple's speech service running inside *this* app. macOS will not let an app
  use the microphone or speech recognition unless the app says why, here - and
  without these two keys it does not even ask the person. WebKit refuses before
  requesting, because requesting without a reason would crash the app, and the
  refusal reaches the page as the Web Speech `network` error. So dictation said
  "needs a connection, and there was none" to people who were online the whole
  time. See dictate.ts.

  The wording is what the permission dialog shows, so it says exactly what
  happens and nothing more flattering: the words go into the message box to be
  read and edited, nothing is sent until the person sends it, and the listening
  is Apple's, which may use Apple's servers.

  The Slides chat's microphone (slidesvoice.ts) is the one place the app
  records audio itself: only while that button is on, sent to the
  transcription service the person set up, and not kept. The microphone
  reason says so, because it is the same permission.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Vylo Editor listens only while you dictate into a message box, or while the microphone in the Slides chat is on. What you say there is sent to the transcription service you set up, to be written down. Nothing is kept.</string>
  <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Vylo Editor turns what you say into text in the message box, for you to read and edit before you send it. Apple's speech recognition does the listening.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
